{"lc": 1, "type": "constructor", "id": ["langchain", "schema", "document", "Document"], "kwargs": {"metadata": {"source": "data\\documents\\doc\\psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "filename": "psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "filepath": "data\\documents\\doc\\psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "page": 1, "title": "psoriasisaustralia.org.au_2026.docx", "year": "2026"}, "page_content": "PASI = C1 + C2 + C3 + C4\n\nThe final PASI score can range from 0 to 72.\u00a0\n\n\u00a00 to 5: none to mild psoriasis\n\n6 to 10: Moderate psoriasis\n\n11 or above: Severe psoriasis\n\nPsoriasis Australia provides the information on this website for information purposes only and it does not constitute medical advice. We recommend seeking medical advice from a trained health professional specific to your needs.\n\nTreatment\n\nAlthough there is no cure for psoriasis, the condition can be managed by a wide variety of treatment options depending on the severity of your condition.\n\nThere is no cure for psoriasis, but it can be treated and managed.\n\nEach person\u2019s experience of psoriasis is unique to them, and a treatment that works for one person might not be the right treatment for you. Therefore, treating psoriasis can be a frustrating process as it is trial and error to find the right treatment for the severity of your condition.\n\nMost people with psoriasis can be treated by their GP, however, because psoriasis is more than skin deep, we recommend seeking a referral to a dermatologist who may, depending on the severity our the psoriasis, may prescribe more targeted treatment options.", "type": "Document"}}